s_ssl_set_client_transport_id()\0A * \5Cparam ilen     Length of info in bytes\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         The callback must return 0 if cookie is valid,\0A *                 or a negative error code.\0A */\0Atypedef int mbedtls_ssl_cookie_check_t(void *ctx,\0A                                       const unsigned char *cookie, size_t clen,\0A                                       const unsigned char *info, size_t ilen);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_DTLS_HELLO_VERIFY) && defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_SRV_C)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief           Register callbacks for DTLS cookies\0A *                  (Server only. DTLS only.)\0A *\0A *                  Default: dummy callbacks that fail, in order to force you to\0A *                  register working callbacks (and initialize their context).\0A *\0A *                  To disable HelloVerifyRequest, register NULL callbacks.\0A *\0A * \5Cwarning         Disabling hello verification allows your server to be used\0A *                  for amplification in DoS attacks against other hosts.\0A *                  Only disable if you known this can't happen in your\0A *                  particular environment.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote            See comments on \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_handshake() about handling\0A *                  the MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_HELLO_VERIFY_REQUIRED that is expected\0A *                  on the first handshake attempt when this is enabled.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote            This is also necessary to handle client reconnection from\0A *                  the same port as described in RFC 6347 section 4.2.8 (only\0A *                  the variant with cookies is supported currently). See\0A *                  comments on \5Cc mbedtls_ssl_read() for details.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf              SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam f_cookie_write    Cookie write callback\0A * \5Cparam f_cookie_check    Cookie check callback\0A * \5Cparam p_cookie          Context for both callbacks\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_conf_dtls_cookies(mbedtls_ssl_config *con